  "statement": "Find a nonzero monic polynomial \\(P(x)\\) with integer coefficients and minimal degree such that \\(P(1-\\sqrt[3]{2}+\\sqrt[3]{4})=0\\). (A polynomial is called monic if its leading coefficient is 1.)",
  "answerChoices": [],
  "answer": {
    "letter": null,
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.hmmt-feb-guts-2014-p12.hmmt",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "\\(x^{3}-3 x^{2}+9 x-9\\) Note that \\((1-\\sqrt[3]{2}+\\sqrt[3]{4})(1+\\sqrt[3]{2})=3\\), so \\(1-\\sqrt[3]{2}+\\sqrt[3]{4}=\\frac{3}{1+\\sqrt[3]{2}}\\).\nNow, if \\(f(x)=x^{3}-2\\), we have \\(f(\\sqrt[3]{2})=0\\), so if we let \\(g(x)=f(x-1)=(x-1)^{3}-2=x^{3}-3 x^{2}+3 x-3\\), then \\(g(1+\\sqrt[3]{2})=f(\\sqrt[3]{2})=0\\). Finally, we let \\(h(x)=g\\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right)=\\frac{27}{x^{3}}-\\frac{27}{x^{2}}+\\frac{9}{x}-3\\) so \\(h\\left(\\frac{3}{1+\\sqrt[3]{2}}\\right)=g(1+\\sqrt[3]{2})=0\\). To make this a monic polynomial, we multiply \\(h(x)\\) by \\(-\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\) to get \\(x^{3}-3 x^{2}+9 x-9\\).",
